let's talk about why the war in ukraine  is causing a global food crisis  ukraine is one of the world's biggest  exporters of green essentials like wheat  and corn but right now around 20 million  tons of grain are stuck there  and that's causing serious problems in  parts of the world that depend on that  supply like the middle east and africa  food shortage is not just real but it's  pushing people to the brink of  starvation there is a real risk that  multiple famines will be declared in  2022  and 2023 could be even worse so why is  all that grain stuck how is it affecting  the rest of the world and what's being  done to try to solve the problem  [Music]  ukraine is a huge country with a lot of  really fertile farmland in normal times  it grows enough to feed 400 million  people a year that makes ukraine a major  player in global food markets for  example it's the fifth biggest exporter  of wheat in the world  more than sixty percent of lebanon's  wheat imports come from ukraine in  somalia it's almost fifty percent and in  tunisia it's more than thirty-six  ukraine used to export around 6 million  tons of grain every month almost all of  that got shipped out through its big  seaports which provided a direct route  to the middle east and africa but the  war has changed everything russia has  blockaded the ukrainian coastline it's  taken control of some of the ports and  it's bombed others while the ukrainians  have planted mines in the sea as a  defense  so those major ports in places like  odessa and goliath can't ship anything  in or out it's just too dangerous  and because nothing's moving there's a  huge pileup of grain which is causing  even more problems the storage silos are  full there's no space for this year's  harvest which is happening now and  farmers aren't getting paid  legit  we cannot sell we are not bringing money  back home and we don't have  cash to to produce next  season's crop so with ukraine's sea  routes off the table right now people  have been trying to find alternatives to  get the grain out  some of it's being rerouted to ports in  poland or romania taken by train or  truck  or through smaller ukrainian ports on  the danube river their new ports were  unprepared they were tiny they were  never supposed to ship that much of rain  out of the country and that's just one  issue there are other big practical  challenges like finding enough trucks  and drivers the high cost of fuel and  the fact that ukraine's railway tracks  are wider than in most of europe so they  have to unload and reload the trains at  the border it's really  the merit team shipping that is key i  mean it used to handle 90 percent of the  ukrainian commodity export and that  cannot be compensated by other means of  transport and we can see that in the  numbers ukraine's gone from exporting 6  million tons of grain every month to  just 200 000 in march and it managed to  export about 1 million last month  a bit better but still a drastic drop  compared to before  a drop that people around the world are  feeling to keep the porch closed  as the harvest season is now  coming in in ukraine in july and august  it means a declaration of war on global  food security so what's being done to  try to open those ports well short of  ending the war itself the main option  people are talking about is creating a  safe sea corridor to allow the grain out  possibly enforced by another country or  the u.n  turkey has been trying to get the  negotiations going but both russia and  ukraine would need to agree on the terms  and let's face it we're talking about  two countries at war so of course both  sides don't trust each other  [Music]  progressive  [Music]  [Music]  adding to the distress is the issue of  stolen grain ukraine accuses russia of  taking its grain from the areas it  occupies and then selling it to other  countries something that russia denies  turkey recently seized a russian ship  suspected of carrying stolen grain but  released it a few days later ukraine and  its western allies are putting pressure  on countries not to buy any ukrainian  grain that's shipped out by russia but  when supplies are short and prices are  sky high this leaves many countries with  very hard choices africans in the  assessment  it is a choice between expressing  solidarity with ukraine on one hand and  you know risking the almost  immunostarvation of their populations  and when it comes to that choice i think  africans are going to pursue a path that  is going to ensure they get the food  supplies food is a basic need and you  don't moralize when you're starving  there are so many ripple effects from  the war but it's hard to overstate just  how big the impact of this grain crisis  really is  it's not just about shortages it's also  about prices everywhere going up because  of the disruption to the global food  markets you've also got the rising price  of fertilizer and fuel and then there's  climate change  right now long droughts are affecting  food production in places like east  africa and south america this crisis is  not happening in a vacuum so the world  is already dealing with the ongoing  corvette 19 pandemic and that means that  i've had a massive economic fallout we  have a situation where governments are  tapped out incomes are depressed and so  we were already in a difficult situation  and russia's invasion of ukraine has  only made it all worse  in case you missed it check out our  recent episode all about the russian  president vladimir putin we've linked it  here  [Music]
 

